Irritable bowel syndrome
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Recurrent abdominal pain related to defecation, Altered bowel habits, Abdominal bloating, Absence of alarm features

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-040
Reason for Visit: Severe, agonizing abdominal cramps and massive bloating following an extremely stressful work week, leading to intense patient anxiety regarding a potential bowel obstruction.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient only.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute, severe exacerbation of generalized abdominal cramping and immense visible bloating causing extreme distress and anxiety. Urgent out-of-hours physical assessment was strictly necessary to definitively exclude an acute mechanical bowel obstruction or inflammatory peritonitis, and to provide potent antispasmodic relief and reassurance to de-escalate the acute crisis and prevent an unnecessary emergency department presentation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My stomach is cramping violently, it is blown up like a balloon, and I alternate between severe constipation and sudden diarrhoea."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a 3-year history of recurrent abdominal pain and cramping occurring at least 3 days per week on average. Reports that the relief or exacerbation of abdominal pain is directly related to the act of defecation. Notes a frequent change in the frequency and physical appearance of bowel movements, characterizing a mixed subtype (alternating constipation and diarrhoea). Reports a constant sensation of incomplete rectal evacuation. Today, following a period of intense psychological stress at work, patient developed severe, visible abdominal bloating, subjective severe distension, and agonizing cramps. Notes the passage of clear mucus per rectum. There is a complete and absolute absence of clinical alarm features such as overt rectal bleeding, nocturnal symptoms awakening the patient, or unintentional significant weight loss.
Functional Status: Ambulating independently but frequently bending over clutching the abdomen during spasms.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe acute health anxiety; fearful of a catastrophic diagnosis.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Patient appears highly anxious, visibly uncomfortable, and frequently massaging the abdomen.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is visibly distended and globally tympanic to percussion. Diffuse, mild tenderness present on deep palpation, particularly in the left lower quadrant along the descending colon. Absolute absence of guarding, rigidity, or localized rebound peritonism. Bowel sounds are active and normal-pitched. Rectal examination is deferred based on the benign abdominal exam and absence of alarm features. The clinical presentation explicitly aligns with the tetrad: recurrent abdominal pain related to defecation, altered bowel habits, abdominal bloating, and an absence of alarm features; normal baseline bloods and negative coeliac serology are presumed given the chronic 3-year history.
Environmental Observations: Normal living environment; patient has a heat pack applied to the abdomen.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Irritable Bowel Syndrome (Mixed Subtype) - Status: Exacerbated (Acute severe spastic flare triggered by psychological stress)
Differentials:

Inflammatory Bowel Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The complete absence of nocturnal symptoms, overt rectal bleeding, fever, or unintentional weight loss, and the fact that symptoms are strongly linked to psychological stress rather than insidious, progressive structural damage.

Bowel Obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The passage of stool and flatus today, active normal-pitched bowel sounds, and a soft, non-rigid abdomen upon palpation.

Coeliac Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of profound lethargy, refractory iron deficiency anaemia, or characteristic dermatitis herpetiformis, though formal negative serology is required for definitive long-term exclusion if not previously performed.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, severe exacerbation of chronic functional bowel symptoms, directly triggered by profound psychological stress. The severity of the cramping and distension mandated urgent out-of-hours evaluation to definitively exclude a surgical crisis. Clinical examination firmly established severe functional gaseous distension and colonic spasm (tympanitic, soft abdomen with normal bowel sounds) and definitively excluded peritonism, obstruction, or mass. The explicit absence of 'red flag' features allowed the clinician to safely de-escalate the emergency, preventing an unnecessary and highly costly presentation to the Emergency Department overnight. Targeted antispasmodic therapy and firm reassurance were safely initiated.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Hyoscine Butylbromide (Buscopan) 20mg immediately, then QID PRN for acute spasms.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply a hot water bottle specifically to the lower left side of your abdomen and practice deep, slow diaphragmatic breathing (expanding your belly, not your chest) for 10 minutes. This combination of localized heat and deep breathing physically interrupts the hyperactive nerve signals causing the severe muscle spasms in your colon, directly overriding the stress response and relaxing the bowel wall.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks to discuss long-term management strategies, stress reduction techniques, and dietary review.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the abdominal pain becomes sudden, continuous, and unbearable (no longer coming in waves), if the abdomen becomes hard like a board, or if you begin vomiting repeatedly, present immediately to the nearest Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: IBS is a functional disorder, meaning the bowel looks normal but functions abnormally due to hypersensitive nerves.
Clinical Course: The extreme cramping will subside completely within the next few hours with the antispasmodic medication and stress reduction.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The physical examination today showed no signs of a blocked or burst bowel. However, if you develop a high fever, start passing fresh blood in the toilet, or if the pain wakes you from a deep sleep, you must seek medical care as these are not symptoms of IBS.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your bowel is structurally completely healthy, but the nerves controlling the muscle wall are highly sensitive to stress. The intense psychological stress you experienced this week triggered an overwhelming 'fight or flight' response, causing the smooth muscle in your colon to violently spasm and trap normal digestive gas. The prescribed medication works directly on these nerves to paralyze the spasm, instantly releasing the trapped gas and stopping the agonizing cramps.
Actionable Steps: Eliminate all caffeine and highly processed foods for the next 48 hours. Caffeine is a powerful direct stimulant to the bowel muscle; consuming it while your nerves are already hypersensitive will immediately trigger further violent spasms and worsen the diarrhoea.

